      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800" open>
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            How much does a torque converter weigh?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          A standard passenger vehicle torque converter typically weighs between 25 and 45 pounds (11.3 to 20.4 kg) when dry. Compact or aftermarket racing torque converters can weigh as little as 18 to 22 pounds, whereas heavy-duty commercial truck torque converters (such as Allison units) frequently exceed 50 to 60 pounds. Retained automatic transmission fluid (ATF) adds another 3 to 6 pounds to these figures.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            How much does a 4L60E torque converter weigh?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          An original factory stock GM 4L60E torque converter (300 mm diameter) weighs approximately 31 to 33 pounds (14.1 to 15 kg) when completely dry. When filled with automatic transmission fluid during active operation, total weight increases to between 38 and 42 pounds (17.2 to 19.1 kg). Smaller aftermarket high-stall performance converters for the 4L60E (such as 258 mm models) generally weigh between 23 and 26 pounds dry.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            What is the weight of a catalytic converter?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          Most passenger car catalytic converters weigh between 8 and 20 pounds (3.6 to 9.1 kg), depending on case size and whether the exhaust flange or pipe section is attached. Large SUV and light truck converters typically weigh 15 to 25 pounds, while commercial diesel catalytic converters and diesel particulate filter (DPF) units can weigh between 30 and 50+ pounds (13.6 to 23 kg) due to thick stainless steel casing and dense ceramic monolith cores.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            How do you convert kg to lbs quickly in your head?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          Double the kilogram value, then add 10 percent of that doubled number. For example, to convert 60 kg to pounds: double 60 to get 120, then add 10 percent (12) to arrive at 132 lbs. The exact mathematical calculation is 132.28 lbs, meaning the mental shortcut is accurate to within a quarter of a percent.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            How many kilograms are in a pound?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          There are exactly 0.45359237 kilograms in one international avoirdupois pound. To convert any pound figure to kilograms with high precision, multiply the pound value by 0.45359237, or divide the pound value by 2.20462262.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            Why does residual fluid alter automotive converter shipping weight?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          Torque converters operate via internal fluid hydrodynamics, using automatic transmission fluid that circulates through tightly packed internal fins and stator assemblies. Because the internal design traps fluid behind one-way clutches and curved impellers, standard gravity draining leaves between 1 and 2 quarts of ATF inside. Since one quart of transmission fluid weighs approximately 1.85 pounds (0.84 kg), trapped fluid routinely pushes packaged parcel weights over courier shipping thresholds.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            What is the difference between a standard ounce and a Troy ounce?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          A standard avoirdupois ounce equals 28.3495 grams, whereas a Troy ounce equals 31.1035 grams. Precious metals like gold, silver, and platinum are exclusively weighed and traded in Troy ounces, which are roughly 9.7 percent heavier than everyday food or grocery ounces.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            How do you convert body weight between stones, pounds, and kilograms?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          One stone equals exactly 14 pounds (approximately 6.3503 kilograms). To convert a compound weight such as 11 stone 4 pounds to total pounds, multiply 11 by 14 (154 lbs) and add 4 to reach 158 pounds. To convert that to kilograms, multiply 158 by 0.45359 to reach 71.67 kg.
        </p>
      </details>
